		<label>66-136 LMG</label>
		<description>Caliber: 7.62x39mm. 75-round detachable mag. Select-Fire.

The "66-136" is a Chinese prototype LMG (and an assault rifle) developed in the mid-1960s. Starting early in the decade, the PLA administration began looking for weapons to replace the Type 56 AK and SKS rifles, which were satisfactory in general performance, but suffered from barrel life issues. The first potential successor emerged in the Type 63 assault rifle, which was ultimately adopted, but procurement was slow because of bad quality in the mass production lines. The program that produced the 66-136 rifle followed in 1966, as it was believed that a new short-recoil system could significantly improve the reliability of weapons in service. However, neither the Type 63 nor 66-136 rifle had a chance to be very successful due to internal conflict in China, and development of the 66-136 ceased before 1970.</description>
